-- title:
--   Coordinatewise bound implies a bound on the maximum norm
-- statement:
--   Let $x \in \mathbb{R}^d$ and let $c \in \mathbb{R}$ satisfy $c \ge 0$. If $|x_i| \le c$ for every coordinate index $i$, then
--   $$\|x\|_\infty \;\le\; c,
--   \qquad\text{where}\quad \|x\|_\infty = \sup_i |x_i| .$$
--
--   This is the introduction rule for the maximum norm: it is how membership of a cube $\{ \|x-u\|_\infty \le h/2\}$ is established from coordinatewise information, which is the form in which such information usually arises.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The hypothesis $c \ge 0$ is needed because $\|x\|_\infty$ is defined as an indexed supremum, which in the degenerate case $d = 0$ is the supremum of the empty family and equals $0$; there the coordinatewise hypothesis is vacuous and only $0 \le c$ gives the conclusion.
